WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:04.500
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:08.490
Scientists at Google just released the Translatotron.

00:00:08.490 --> 00:00:14.769
This is an AI that is able to translate speech
from one language into speech into another

00:00:14.769 --> 00:00:20.890
language, and here comes the first twist,
without using text as an intermediate representation.

00:00:20.890 --> 00:00:25.199
You give it the soundwaves, and you get the
translated soundwaves.

00:00:25.199 --> 00:00:30.169
And this neural network was trained approximately
on a million voice samples.

00:00:30.169 --> 00:00:34.480
So let’s see what learning on this one million
samples gives us.

00:00:34.480 --> 00:00:35.480
Listen.

00:00:35.480 --> 00:00:44.030
This is the input sentence in Spanish.

00:00:44.030 --> 00:01:11.320
And here it is, translated to English, but
using the voice of the same person.

00:01:11.320 --> 00:01:12.320
This is incredible.

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:18.030
However, there is another twist, perhaps an
even bigger one, believe it or not.

00:01:18.030 --> 00:01:22.960
This technique can not only translate, but
can also perform voice transfer, so it can

00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:26.280
say the same thing using someone else’s
voice.

00:01:26.280 --> 00:01:31.460
This means that the AI not only has to learn
what to say, but how to say it.

00:01:31.460 --> 00:01:33.340
This is immensely difficult.

00:01:33.340 --> 00:01:38.320
It’s also not that easy to know what we
need to listen to and when, so let me walk

00:01:38.320 --> 00:01:39.549
you through it.

00:01:39.549 --> 00:01:45.850
This is a source sentence in Spanish.

00:01:45.850 --> 00:01:55.200
This is the same sentence said by someone
else, an actual person, and in English.

00:01:55.200 --> 00:02:03.619
And now, the same thing, but synthesized by
the algorithm using both of their voices.

00:02:03.619 --> 00:02:14.080
Let’s listen to them side by side some more.

00:02:14.080 --> 00:02:32.850
This is so good, let’s have a look at some
more examples.

00:02:32.850 --> 00:02:46.960
Wow.

00:02:46.960 --> 00:02:52.590
The method performs the learning by trying
to map these Mel spectrograms between multiple

00:02:52.590 --> 00:02:53.590
speakers.

00:02:53.590 --> 00:02:58.490
You can see example sentences here and their
corresponding spectrograms, which are concise

00:02:58.490 --> 00:03:02.570
representations of someone’s voice and intonation.

00:03:02.570 --> 00:03:08.010
And of course, it is difficult to mathematically
formalize what makes a good translation and

00:03:08.010 --> 00:03:13.270
a good mimicking of someone’s voice, so
in these cases, we let people be the judge

00:03:13.270 --> 00:03:18.431
and have them listen to a few speech signals
and asking them to guess which was a real

00:03:18.431 --> 00:03:21.820
person, and which was the AI speaking.

00:03:21.820 --> 00:03:26.710
If you take a closer look at the paper, you
will see that it smokes the competition.

00:03:26.710 --> 00:03:32.100
This is great progress on an immensely difficult
task as we have to perform proper translation

00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:35.180
and voice transfer at the same time.

00:03:35.180 --> 00:03:37.190
It’s quite a challenge.

00:03:37.190 --> 00:03:55.470
Of course, failure cases still exist.

00:03:55.470 --> 00:04:11.570
Listen.

00:04:11.570 --> 00:04:16.680
Just imagine that you are in a foreign country
and all you need to do is use your phone to

00:04:16.680 --> 00:04:23.400
tell stories to people not only in their own
language, but also using your own voice, even

00:04:23.400 --> 00:04:26.620
if you don’t speak a word of their language.

00:04:26.620 --> 00:04:27.699
Beautiful.

00:04:27.699 --> 00:04:32.949
Even this video could perhaps be available
in a variety of languages using my own voice

00:04:32.949 --> 00:04:39.069
within the next few years, although I wonder
how these algorithms would pronounce my name.

00:04:39.069 --> 00:04:43.650
So far, that proved to be quite a challenge
for humans and AIs alike.

00:04:43.650 --> 00:04:47.259
And for now, all hail the mighty Translatotron.

00:04:47.259 --> 00:04:51.250
